Subject: [PATCH] canvas: noise moves a pixel inside its band, the same on every route

CreepJS reported "pixel data modified": random pixels written with
putImageData came back about five bytes off, and a canvas read through
toDataURL, decoded and drawn again differed from a direct read by about
1300 bytes. Stock Chrome gives 0 for both.

Each 8-bit channel value now falls in a band of up to four values whose
position is the profile's own; a first band holds 0 and the faintest
values. Only an anti-aliased pixel moves -- unlike its four direct
neighbours, or among three or more values in its 3x3 neighbourhood,
judged on bands -- and it moves inside its band, keyed on the seed and
the bands around it in any order. Noise applied to noised pixels changes
nothing, so a read written back reads back unchanged, an encoded copy
decoded and read again equals the direct read, a read of part of a
canvas agrees with a read of all of it, and a bottom-up WebGL read agrees
with the same pixels read top-down. getImageData, toDataURL, toBlob,
convertToBlob (also in workers), ImageBitmap copies and WebGL readPixels
give the same pixels. Alpha moves only where alpha varies and never off 0
or 255; colour moves only in pixels at least half opaque, and a zero or a
full channel stays as it is. Hashes of rounded values, of alpha and of
where alpha is non-zero still differ between profiles.

A 2D canvas that holds only what the page wrote itself -- putImageData,
pixel-aligned opaque solid rectangles, clears -- reads back exactly, and
so does any canvas or drawing buffer under 16 pixels on a side. An RGBA
readPixels reads the framebuffer again with a one-pixel border, so its
edge pixels key as in a read of the whole buffer. Signed, float and wider
stores keep a one-step rule.

